                  "content": "SELECTED: candidate_13\n\nCONFIDENCE: HIGH\n\nEXPLANATION:\nThe fix commit's diff to TableFactoryRenderer.java changes a generated line from:\n    line(\"return \", Table, \".of(t, name, namespace, Triggers.getAllTriggers(t, sharedTriggers, triggers));\");\nto:\n    line(\"return \", Table, \".of(t, namespace, name, Triggers.getAllTriggers(t, sharedTriggers, triggers));\");\n\nThis corresponds exactly to the commit message note \"fixed generated table factory method argument ordering\": the generated static factory method for \"generic\" tables (those with a getGenericTableName()) is called as `Table.of(t, namespace, name, triggers)`, but the code-generator (TableFactoryRenderer) was emitting a call with `name` and `namespace` swapped, i.e. `Table.of(t, name, namespace, triggers)`.\n\nSearching the candidates for where this exact buggy line was introduced, candidate_13 is the commit that first adds the `namespace` parameter to `TableFactoryRenderer.getTable()` and generates the code:\n    _(\"return \", Table, \".of(t, name, namespace, Triggers.getAllTriggers(t, sharedTriggers, triggers));\");\n(candidate_13.diff, TableFactoryRenderer.java hunk, lines ~98-101).\n\nBefore candidate_13 (e.g. candidate_01), the generated call was `Table.of(t, name, Triggers...)` \u2014 there was no `namespace` argument at all, so the ordering bug could not yet exist. Candidate_13 is the commit that adds the namespace parameter but puts it in the wrong position relative to `name` in the generated `.of(...)` call, introducing the argument-ordering bug that persists through later candidates (e.g. candidate_16, which only renames the `_` method to `line` without correcting the order) until it is finally fixed in the target fix commit by swapping to `.of(t, namespace, name, ...)`.\n\nBecause the actual generated table classes' `of(...)` static factory methods take `(Transaction, Namespace, String name, ...)` (namespace before name), the TableFactoryRenderer-generated caller code introduced in candidate_13 has arguments in the wrong order, causing incorrect namespace/name values to be passed at runtime for generic (dynamically-named) tables \u2014 exactly the bug the fix commit corrects.\n"
                }
              }
            ]
          },
